# Python and Django

- Follow the supported Python/Django versions and repository tooling.
  Use idiomatic Python and PEP 8 where project configuration leaves choices open.
- Inspect the closest Django app before creating a model or module.
  Match its organization of models, managers/querysets, forms/serializers,
  views, services, URLs, admin and tests where applicable.
- Place business logic according to established responsibilities.
  Do not introduce service/repository layers or move logic into signals
  without a concrete need.
- Reuse shared models, mixins, validators and queryset methods when suitable.
  Do not force unrelated domain behavior into a shared abstraction.
- Enforce validation and data integrity at appropriate boundaries;
  use database constraints where required.
- Verify permissions, object-level access and tenant isolation when applicable.
- Handle atomic operations and concurrency risks explicitly when relevant.
- Review schema and data migrations for existing data, compatibility,
  reversibility where feasible and deployment impact.
- Check ORM access patterns for N+1 queries, unnecessary evaluation and
  excessive loading. Use select_related/prefetch_related when justified.
- Add indexes based on query patterns and constraints, considering write costs.
- Test affected behavior and relevant validation, permission and integrity cases.